# <type>(<scope>): <subject in the imperative, at most 72 characters>
#
# <body: explain what changed and why, wrapped at 72 characters. Describe
# the mathematics when behavior changes, and state the old behavior next to
# the new one so that a reviewer can judge the difference without running
# the code. Leave one blank line between the subject and the body.>
#
# <footer: issue references and breaking change notes>
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# This is the commit message template for discretus. Enable it once with:
#
#     git config commit.template .gitmessage
#
# Every line that starts with a hash is stripped before the message is
# stored, so this guidance never reaches the history.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Type, required. Choose exactly one:
#
#   feat      a new structure, algorithm, or public interface
#   fix       a correction to behavior that was wrong
#   perf      a change that improves speed or memory without changing
#             documented behavior
#   refactor  an internal change with no behavioral effect
#   docs      documentation, docstrings, tutorials, or examples
#   test      tests only, including a regression test for a known defect
#   build     packaging, dependencies, or the build backend
#   ci        continuous integration and repository automation
#   chore     maintenance that fits none of the above
#   revert    an undo of an earlier commit, naming it in the body
#
# Scope, optional but preferred. Name the package the change belongs to:
#
#   core, sets, logic, combinatorics, graphs, number_theory, recurrences,
#   algebra, viz, io, utils, cli, docs, benchmarks, scripts
#
# Subject rules:
#
#   Use the imperative mood, as in "add", "fix", "remove", not "added" or
#   "adds". Do not capitalize the first word after the colon. Do not end
#   with a period. Keep it under 72 characters so that it is never
#   truncated in a log listing.
#
# Body rules:
#
#   State the reason for the change, not only its content, because the
#   diff already shows the content. Mention the asymptotic complexity of a
#   new routine, the reference you followed for an algorithm, and the way
#   you validated the result, for example cross validation against a naive
#   implementation on randomized inputs.
#
# Footer rules:
#
#   Reference issues with "Closes #123" or "Refs #123". Announce an
#   incompatible change with a "BREAKING CHANGE:" paragraph that names the
#   affected interface and gives the migration step.
#
# Prose rules that apply to every message:
#
#   Use plain punctuation. Do not use the long dash character and do not
#   use emoji anywhere in the repository, including commit messages.
#
# Example:
#
#   feat(graphs): add Boruvka minimum spanning tree
#
#   Implement the Boruvka algorithm with union find component merging,
#   which contracts every component in a round and therefore runs in
#   O(|E| log |V|) time. The result is cross validated against Kruskal on
#   ten thousand randomized weighted graphs, since both must return the
#   same total weight even when the trees themselves differ.
#
#   Closes #42
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# One example per type, so that a contributor can copy the closest one
# rather than inventing a shape.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   feat(number_theory): add Tonelli Shanks modular square roots
#
#   Solve x squared congruent to a modulo an odd prime p by the Tonelli
#   Shanks algorithm, which reduces to the cyclic structure of the
#   multiplicative group and runs in O(log squared p) modular
#   multiplications for a prime where the two part of p minus one is
#   small. The routine returns both roots, or reports that a is a
#   quadratic non residue, which the Legendre symbol decides first.
#
#   Validated against exhaustive search for every prime below ten
#   thousand and every residue, which is the strongest check available
#   at that size.
#
#   Closes #118
#
#   fix(graphs): reject negative cycles in Bellman Ford instead of looping
#
#   The relaxation loop ran the documented number of rounds and then
#   returned the distances it had, which are meaningless when a negative
#   cycle is reachable from the source. It now performs the extra round
#   the algorithm prescribes and raises InfeasibleError naming one vertex
#   on the cycle, so a caller can locate the problem in the input.
#
#   The previous behavior silently produced wrong shortest paths, which
#   is worse than an error, so this is a correction and not a feature.
#
#   Closes #204
#
#   perf(logic): watch two literals per clause in the DPLL solver
#
#   Replace the linear scan over every clause at each decision with the
#   two watched literal scheme, which visits a clause only when one of
#   its two watches is falsified. On the pigeonhole instances in the
#   benchmark suite the change reduces propagation time by about eighty
#   percent and leaves the returned models unchanged.
#
#   refactor(sets): move the closure algorithms onto the relation base
#
#   The reflexive, symmetric, and transitive closures were implemented
#   separately in the relation, order, and equivalence modules, which had
#   already drifted in their handling of an empty ground set. They now
#   live once on RelationBase and the three modules delegate to it. No
#   public interface changes.
#
#   docs(algebra): state the orbit stabilizer theorem in the action module
#
#   The module implemented the theorem without stating it, which left a
#   reader to infer the invariant from the code. Add the statement, the
#   complexity of the orbit computation, and a worked example over the
#   symmetric group on four letters.
#
#   test(combinatorics): cross validate ranking against enumeration
#
#   Assert that unranking the index of every permutation of up to seven
#   letters returns the original permutation, and that the ranks of the
#   enumerated permutations are exactly the integers below the factorial.
#   Together these pin the bijection in both directions.
#
#   build(deps): raise the minimum supported interpreter to 3.9
#
#   The typing constructs the library uses are unavailable on 3.8, which
#   reached end of life. Drop it from the matrix, raise requires-python,
#   and remove the two compatibility branches it required.
#
#   BREAKING CHANGE: Python 3.8 is no longer supported. Upgrade the
#   interpreter, or pin discretus below this release.
#
#   ci(lint): reject long dash characters and emoji in the prose job
#
#   The repository writes prose with plain punctuation. Add a check that
#   scans every text file and fails on a long dash or an emoji, so that
#   the convention is enforced rather than remembered.
#
#   chore: remove the unused benchmark fixture directory
#
#   The directory has been empty since the benchmark suite moved to
#   generated inputs, and it confused new contributors looking for the
#   fixtures the old documentation mentioned.
#
#   revert: undo "perf(sets): cache the power set enumeration"
#
#   This reverts commit 0123456789abcdef, which cached the enumeration
#   across calls and therefore held the whole power set in memory,
#   defeating the lazy generator the module documents. The performance
#   gain it reported came from measuring the cached second call.
